  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Funny River got its name in the 1950s when a local pilot jokingly called the winding river path 'funny' while flying over it.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.trawellino.com/destinations/united-states/alaska_funny-river",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 30‑mile Funny River flows northwest, joining the Kenai River about 11 miles east of Soldotna, where the Kenai River marks Funny River’s northern boundary.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/funny_river_alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The area that is now Funny River was long part of the traditional territory of the Dena’ina people, an Indigenous Athabascan group.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/funny_river_alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In May 2014, a human‑caused fire near Funny River Road burned roughly 200,000 acres, making it Alaska’s largest wildfire that year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/funny_river_alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "After the 2014 fire, agencies such as the U.S. Forest Service led extensive reforestation and habitat restoration efforts around Funny River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/funny_river_alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Visitors can fish for salmon and trout in the Kenai River, spot moose and bears along Funny River Road, and camp at the 10‑site Funny River Campground.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
